[38;5;231m⏺[39m Greenfield it is. That makes this bigger than one spec, so I'll flag the shape now and then narrow.

  What you've described is really three subsystems stacked:

  1. [1mTask core[0m — what a task is, CRUD, persistence. Nothing else can exist without it.
  2. [1mIdentity & interest[0m — who a user is, and what "cares about" means (assignee? watcher? creator? project member?).
  3. [1mNotifications[0m — detect a change, decide who's interested, deliver it, track read state.

  Each needs its own spec → plan → build cycle, and 3 depends entirely on 1 and 2. I'd design them in that order and
  brainstorm only the first one fully today. But there's one question upstream of even that decomposition, because it
  decides whether a notification system is a real feature or an empty one.

  [1mThe fork:[0m in a single-user app, every task change is one you just made yourself — a "task changed" notification tells
  you something you already know, and the only genuinely useful triggers are time-based (due dates, overdue). In a
  multi-user app, changes originate from [3mother people[0m, and change notifications carry real information. These aren't
  variations on one design; they're different systems. Multi-user also drags in a server, accounts, and auth — the
  single-user version can stay entirely in the browser.

  - [1mMulti-user[0m — buys real change notifications and collaboration; costs a backend, accounts, auth, and a delivery
    channel. Expensive to add later if the data model assumed one user.
  - [1mSingle-user + time-based[0m — buys a genuinely useful reminder feature for a fraction of the work, stays client-side;
    rules out "Alice edited your task" until you add a server.
  - [1mSingle-user now, multi-user later[0m — build the task core with a [38;5;153muser_id[39m on every row from day one, ship reminders,
    add collaboration when it's needed. The seam is cheap now and expensive to retrofit.

  My recommendation is the third. Notifications are the last layer of the stack, and the decision you can't cheaply
  reverse isn't the notification design — it's whether the task model has an owner column. Get that right, ship
  something useful, and the collaborative version stays open.

